Add a goal loop: keep going until the thing is actually done

A turn ending means "this step finished", not "the goal is met". goal/set (and
/goal in the REPL) attaches a natural-language condition to a session; when the
main loop stops, a judge decides whether the goal holds, and if not its reason
becomes the next user message and another round runs in the same session.

Three constraints, all borrowed from implementations that learned them:

- The judge is a cheap model with no tools and only the conversation. Its
  question is "does this conversation show the goal met" — give it tools and it
  becomes a second executor whose verdict was supposed to be side-effect free.
- Rounds are capped (JOY_GOAL_MAX_ROUNDS, default 5). Hitting the cap sets
  round-limit and says the goal was NOT met: no pretending, and the goal is not
  cleared, because a record of an unfinished goal is worth more than a clean
  slate.
- Goals are set and cleared by humans only. The model has no tool for it, so
  "the model assigning itself work" is not forbidden here — the path does not
  exist.

The judge's answer is validated: ok and impossible are mutually exclusive, and a
reply that sets both is treated as not-done rather than letting the loop retire
on an answer that contradicts itself. A judge that fails to answer sets blocked,
stops the loop and keeps the goal. Terminal outcomes are recorded on the goal
(Goal.status) so the loop does not re-ask the same question every turn — that
would both cost money and dress up a stopped loop as a running one.

Server::goal_set holds the single implementation (dispatch and the REPL both call
it; two copies would eventually disagree about max_rounds). Every judgement
emits GoalRound — the only answer to "why did it run again" — and the REPL prints
a line for it, including the statuses that mean "gave up", which are exactly the
ones worth reading.

Since the goal loop lives in run_turn rather than in the loop driver, the eval
harness cannot set a goal yet, so the coverage is an end-to-end app-server test
with a real turn: continuing then satisfied, meta.goalRounds == 2, the reason fed
back as the next message, plus round-limit honesty, judge-failure blocking and
the contradiction rule. 5 tests; protocol regenerated (goal/set, GoalRound,
TurnMeta.goalStatus/goalRounds) including the Python constants. Docs:
configuration, protocol, limitations, internals 9, CHANGELOG — EN + zh.
